ME. ''amisse'', ''amysse'', ''a mysse'', ''a mys'', ''o mys'', also ''on mys'', ''of mys'', earliest form ''a mis'' (= Icel. ''ā mis'', ''ā miss''): ''a'', ''o'', ''on'', E. ''a''<sup>3</sup>; ''mis'', E. ''miss''<sup>1</sup>, fault; cf. ME. ''mis'', adv., amiss. See ''miss''<sup>1</sup> and ''mis-''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''adv.'' Away from the mark; out of the way; out of the proper course or order; in a faulty manner; wrongly; in a manner contrary to propriety, truth, law, or morality.
{{smaller block|Ye ask, and receive not, because ye ask ''amiss''. {{right|Jas. iv. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|We read ''amiss'', if we imagine that the fiery persecution which raged against Christ had burned itself out in the act of the crucifixion. {{right|''De Quincey'', Essenes, i.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Improper; wrong; faulty: used only in the predicate: as, it may not be ''amiss'' to ask advice.
{{smaller block|<poem>There’s somewhat in this world ''amiss''
Shall be unriddled by and by.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Miller’s Daughter.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Much I find ''amiss'',
Blameworthy, punishable in this freak
Of thine.</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, II. 202.}}}}
{{smaller block|There is something ''amiss'' in one who has to grope for his theme and cannot adjust himself to his period. {{right|''Stedman'', Vict. Poets, p. 301.}}}}

'''Not amiss,''' {{smaller|passable or suitable; fair; not so bad after all: a phrase used to express approval, but not in a very emphatic way. [Colloq.]}}
{{smaller block|She’s a miss, she is; and yet she ''an’t amiss''—eh? {{right|''Dickens.''}}}}

'''To come amiss,''' {{smaller|to be unwelcome; be not wanted; be out of the proper place or time.}}
{{smaller block|Neyther Religion ''cummeth amisse''. {{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster.}}}}

'''To take amiss,''' {{smaller|to be offended at.}}

