¶
{{smaller block|We desire no records of such enormities; sins should be accounted new, that so they may be esteemed monstrous. They ''amit'' of monstrosity, as they fall from their rarity.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne.''}}}}

'''amity''' (am′i̤-ti), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''amitie'', < OF. ''amitie'', ''amistie'', ''amisted'', ''amistet'' = Sp. ''amistad'' = Pg. ''amizade'' = It. ''amistà'', < ML. *''amicita(t-)s'', friendship, < L. ''amicus'', friendly, a friend: see ''amiable''.] Friendship, in a general sense; harmony; good understanding, especially between nations; political friendship: as, a treaty of ''amity'' and commerce.
{{smaller block|Great Britain was in league and ''amity'' with all the world.
{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Ireland.}}}}
{{smaller block|These appearances and sounds which imply ''amity'' or enmity in those around, become symbolic of happiness and misery.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Prin. of Psychol., § 520.}}}}
{{smaller block|I much prefer the company of ploughboys and tin-peddlers to the silken and perfumed ''amity'' which celebrates its days of encounter by a frivolous display.
{{right|''Emerson'', Friendship.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' Friendliness, kindness, good will, affection, harmony.}}

'''Amiurus''' (am-i-ū′rus), ''n.'' [NL., not curtailed, i. e., with the tail not notched, having the tail even or square; < Gr. ἀ- priv. + μείουρος, curtailed, curtal, < μείων, less (compar. of μικρός, little), + οὐρά, tail.] A large genus of ''Siluridæ'', containing many of the commonest American species of catfishes, horned pouts or bullheads, such as ''A. nebulosus''. {{smaller|There are some 15 species, among them ''A. nigricans'', the great-lake cat, and ''A. ponderosus'', the Mississippi cat, sometimes weighing upward of 100 pounds. Also written ''Ameiurus'', as originally by Rafinesque, 1820. See cut under ''catfish''.}}

